## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical serverless data flow, idempotency, and cost control in Azure. It also touches inter-service communication and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent HTTP endpoints using an idempotency key
- Decoupling with HTTP → Blob → Queue → Worker
- Cosmos DB for idempotency store and metadata
- Retry/backoff and dead-lettering
- Cost-conscious design on Consumption plan

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: HTTP trigger checks idempotency key, writes blob, enqueues, and stores metadata
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const key = req.headers["x-idempotency-key"];
  // look up key in Cosmos DB; if exists, return 200
  // otherwise, save blob, enqueue job, write metadata, and return 202
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency guarantees in this flow?
- What metrics would you collect to validate retry/backoff behavior and cost control?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests crypto maturity, per-tenant isolation, and how encryption keys impact storage systems. It also probes end-to-end tracing, secure key management, and real-world rotation workflows in Azure.

## Key Concepts
- Envelope encryption with per-tenant DEK
- KEK and DEK lifecycle in Azure Key Vault
- Zero-downtime KEK rotation using Durable Functions
- End-to-end tracing across Functions, Cosmos DB, and Data Lake
- Per-env CI/CD and access governance

## Code Example
```javascript
import { DefaultAzureCredential } from "@azure/identity";
import { KeyClient, CryptographyClient } from "@azure/keyvault-keys";

const credential = new DefaultAzureCredential();
const keyClient = new KeyClient("https://<vault>.vault.azure.net", credential);
async function fetchTenantDEK(tenantId) {
  const key = await keyClient.getKey(`dek-${tenantId}`);
  // In practice, wrap/unwrap with a CryptographyClient here
  return key;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate zero-downtime rotation impact on latency?
- How would you audit key access and revoke keys per tenant if needed?

